2026-06-30 15:52:45,583 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-3007368e-6725-4b0e-9724-a5578472611b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to crosslinking reaction conditions and enrichment.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: crosslinking reaction time\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslinking reaction time]\nontology: PRIDE:0000949\ndescription: Duration of the crosslinking reaction.\npattern: number + time unit (e.g. "30 min", "1 h", "2 hours", "overnight")\ninstructions: Extract from Methods. Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinking temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslinking temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000950\ndescription: Temperature at which the crosslinking reaction was performed.\npattern: number + °C (e.g. "25 °C", "4 °C", "37 °C", "room temperature")\ninstructions: Return "room temperature" if stated as such. Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslink enrichment method\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslink enrichment method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000838\ndescription: Method used to enrich for crosslinked peptides prior to LC-MS/MS.\nexamples: size exclusion chromatography (SEC), strong cation exchange chromatography (SCX),\n  high-pH reversed-phase chromatography, FAIMS, affinity enrichment, SEC-SCX,\n  peptide-level SEC, protein-level SEC\ninstructions: Return null if no explicit enrichment step for crosslinked peptides is\n  described (note: standard LC-MS/MS without prior enrichment = null or "not applicable").\n\nterm: enrichment process\nsdrf_column: characteristics[enrichment process]\nontology: EFO:0009090\ndescription: Enrichment strategy applied to the sample (for crosslinking this typically\n  refers to crosslinked peptide/protein enrichment).\ninstructions: Use children of EFO:0009090. Examples: "enrichment by size exclusion",\n  "size-exclusion chromatography enrichment", "no enrichment".\n  Return "no enrichment" if no enrichment step is described.\n  Return null only if completely unclear.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA high-efficiency Azide-A-DSBSO cross-linking-MS protocol for identifying thousands of in vivo protein interactions in single day\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nEnrichable and mass spectrometry (MS)-cleavable crosslinkers have gained popularity in crosslinking-MS (XL-MS) in the past decade. The azide-tagged, acid-cleavable disuccinimidyl bis-sulfoxide (DSBSO) crosslinker offers both these advantages, enabling deep XL-MS interactomics studies in intact cells. However, current DSBSO XL-MS workflows take several days, which limits the experimental throughput. Here, we developed an in-cell DSBSO XL-MS protocol that allows sample ready for MS analysis within 8 hours. By systematically optimizing digestion, click chemistry enrichment, LC parameters, and MS fragmentation, we increased the number of crosslink spectrum matches (CSMs) from single-shot experiments more than twofold compared to previous single-shot measurements. This allowed us to efficiently generate deep protein-protein interaction datasets from intact human mitochondria, Bacillus subtilis, and HEK293T cells.\n\n[METHODS]\nFirst, we apply crosslinking reaction on prepared samples, following by lysis and quickly processed with reduction/alkylation. In digestion buffer, pre-digestion will be performed for one hour before DBCO agarose beads dropped into sample. Owing to the inertness and accuracy of, we boldly make click chemistry reacting in tryptic digestion buffer in next three hours. After beads incubation, beads are washed with strong detergent coupled with high salt buffer in Spin column. At the step of acid cleavage, we use 2% trifluoroacetic acid under 37 degrees Celsius for only one hour instead of stronger acid to reduce troublesome for later steps. For last step, we cleaned sample with C8 Stagetip/SepPak column. Afterward, crosslinked sample can be queued for LC-MS/MS analysis.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
